.TH COORDD 1 "2026-08-02" "logscope" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
coordd \- the coordination daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B coordd
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B coordd
serves coord-core's arbitration API to every app that needs to agree on who owns what.
.PP
It is
.B socket-activated:
systemd creates the socket at login and starts the daemon on the first
connection. There is normally no reason to run it by hand, and doing so while
the socket unit is enabled will fail to bind.
.PP
An app connecting before the daemon is running blocks on the socket rather than
failing, so nothing needs a retry loop or a start-order dependency.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/coord.sock
The socket. Mode 0600 \(em per-user, not shared between accounts on one machine.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/coordd/
State. The only path the service unit leaves writable.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
Clean shutdown.
.TP
.B non-zero
Failure; systemd restarts it after 2 seconds.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR systemd.socket (5)
.PP
Enable the
.B socket,
not the service:
.PP
.RS
systemctl --user enable --now coordd.socket
.RE
.PP
Enabling the service directly starts it at login regardless, which defeats
socket activation and reintroduces the idle process it exists to avoid.
